FIXES 0057, WHICH DID NOT WORK.

0057 used dev_err_ratelimited to silence the "handover" storm that eats the
ramoops console buffer. IT DOES NOT SILENCE IT: the default limit is 10 messages
every 5 seconds, and this emits 1 per second, that is, below the threshold.
Measured: 20 messages in 20 s, the same as before patching.

WHY IT MATTERS
--------------
When the boot fails, the phone stays down for minutes until the watchdog resets
it or someone presses the button. During ALL that time the interrupt keeps
firing once per second, and those hundreds of lines push the beginning of the
boot out of the ring, which is exactly what has to be read. Measured: after
653 s down, the trace started at 11.8 s.

That is: the longer you wait for the warm rescue -the only one that preserves
the memory-, the more of what you want to see gets erased. The two things needed
were destroying each other.

dev_err_once says it once and shuts up. The warning still appears, which is the
useful part, and the ring keeps the whole boot however many minutes go by.

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -165,7 +165,8 @@
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		dev_err_once(q6v5->dev,
+			     "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
